    "body": "### Problem\n\nI started to explore the new listing-dir + its default `:bcd` and found this:\nRe-reading a directory listing (`:edit .`, or `R` after navigating into a subdir) resets the process cwd to the startup directory. A second `:edit .` replaces the listing buffer with the startup directory's contents.\n \n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p /tmp/some-dir && echo '{}' > /tmp/some-dir/serve-latest.json\ncd /tmp\nnvim --clean /tmp/some-dir/serve-latest.json\n```\n\nInside nvim:\n\n```vim\n-          \" open the dir listing\n:pwd       \" -> /tmp/some-dir      (correct)\n:edit .    \" re-read the listing\n:pwd       \" -> /tmp               (BUG: should stay /tmp/some-dir)\n:edit .    \" second re-read\n:ls        \" -> listing switched to /tmp (also part of the bug)\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nRe-reading the listing (`:edit .` / `R`) should not change the cwd or the listing.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n`v0.13.0-dev-1255+g0105f15201` (present since the `:bcd` feature; affects nightly)\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux 6.18.40-2-lts\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.48.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nBuilt from repo (Debug), `master` @ `0105f15201`",
